## Checklist

- [x] This is a feature proposal, not a question, and is written in English.

## Motivation

[Breeze-TTS-2](https://huggingface.co/BreezeBlue/Breeze-TTS-2) is an open-weight bilingual (English/Chinese) TTS model designed for low-latency streaming interaction. It supports:

- reference-audio voice cloning with a reference transcript;
- reference-free voice design from a natural-language instruction;
- voice direction: preserving a reference speaker while steering tone, emotion, pace, and delivery;
- streaming 24 kHz PCM output.

Adding native serving support would extend the current TTS model portfolio with this clone/design/direction workflow while exposing it through the existing OpenAI-compatible `/v1/audio/speech` API.

I am available to lead the initial implementation and validation work.

## Proposed initial scope

Implement a native CUDA/BF16 staged pipeline rather than proxying the upstream single-concurrency FastAPI server:

```text
preprocessing -> autoregressive backbone -> depth decoder -> streaming audio codec
```

The first version would cover:

- `BreezeBlue/Breeze-TTS-2` checkpoint loading;
- English and Chinese synthesis;
- voice cloning (`reference_audio`, `reference_text`);
- voice design and voice direction (`instruction`, `cfg_scale`);
- request-owned `seed` handling;
- streaming 24 kHz PCM through `/v1/audio/speech`;
- request cancellation, stream cleanup, and concurrent-request isolation;
- model tests, a Cookbook page, and H20 benchmark results at concurrency 1, 8, and 32 (TTFA, RTF, end-to-end latency, and VRAM).

The existing Qwen3-TTS pipeline can serve as a structural reference for the request lifecycle and streaming API. Breeze-TTS-2 has its own text encoder, Qwen3 autoregressive backbone, depth decoder, and Mimi codec, so its model execution and token/codec handling would be implemented specifically for this model.

## Out of scope for the initial PR

- Reproducing every upstream CUDA-graph fast path before correctness is established;
- distributed / multi-node execution;
- broad batching or scheduler optimizations beyond a correct native serving baseline;
- changes to the shared API contract unrelated to Breeze-TTS-2.

## Acceptance criteria

- Clone, design, and direction requests produce valid 24 kHz audio for English and Chinese fixtures.
- Streaming output is continuous and request cancellation/disconnect releases model resources correctly.
- Concurrent requests remain isolated, including reference-audio and instruction state.
- The implementation has regression tests and user-facing serving documentation.
- H20 benchmark and profiling results are reported against the upstream reference runtime and relevant existing TTS baselines.

## Related resources

- Model: https://huggingface.co/BreezeBlue/Breeze-TTS-2
- Official inference implementation: https://github.com/breezeblue-ai/breeze-tts
- Upstream model card API example: `python -m breeze_infer.api <model-path>`

## Environment

- flashinfer main `63b24de5` (verified 2026-09-11). The vendored file has not been touched by any commit since #1113 imported it, so the line references below hold for current main.
- Verified on NVIDIA A100-PCIE-40GB (sm_80), driver 575.57.08, nvcc CUDA 12.8.
- The reproduction compiles the vendored CUDA headers directly from a checkout and never imports the flashinfer package, so `python -m flashinfer.collect_env` does not apply here.

## Bug description

The repository carries two copies of TRT-LLM's `cudaTypeUtils.cuh`. The vendored tree under `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/` came in with #1113, and #1212 later created the header-only twin under `include/flashinfer/trtllm/`. PR #4167 fixed the packed fp8 conversion in the twin on 2026-07-27, after #4160 reported intermittent `test_layernorm_quant` failures. The fix never crossed to the vendored copy, which still carries the pre-fix conversion at `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/common/cudaTypeUtils.cuh:595`.

```cpp
template <>
__device__ inline __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 cuda_cast<__nv_fp8x2_e4m3, float2>(float2 val) {
  return __nv_fp8x2_e4m3(bf1622float2(float22bf162(val)));
}
```

This rounds the f32 value to bf16 first and converts to e4m3 afterwards, so every value passes through two roundings. Quantization kernels apply the scale before the cast, and the mantissa bits dropped by the bf16 step are then amplified by 1/scale. That can push the value across an e4m3 rounding boundary, and the result lands one representable step away from the directly rounded value. It is the failure #4160 caught in the twin, where quant_scale=0.01 pushed the mismatch fraction of some seeds past the 1% bound of the nightly check. The fixed twin converts directly.

```cpp
// include/flashinfer/trtllm/common/cudaTypeUtils.cuh:594-599
template <>
__device__ inline __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 cuda_cast<__nv_fp8x2_e4m3, float2>(float2 val) {
  // Direct conversion: an intermediate bf16 step drops mantissa bits that a
  // small quantization scale then amplifies past e4m3 bucket boundaries.
  __nv_fp8x2_e4m3 out;
  out.__x = __nv_cvt_float2_to_fp8x2(val, __NV_SATFINITE, __NV_E4M3);
  return out;
}
```

Diffing the two files on current main shows only the include prefixes, this conversion, and two e5m2 specializations that exist only in the twin.

Inside flashinfer, the fp8 e4m3 instantiation of this cast is currently dormant. The chain is `perTokenQuantization` (`csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/kernels/quantization.cuh:132`) calling `quantizeAndStore`, whose `cuda_cast<QuantT>(val2)` at `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/kernels/quantization_utils.cuh:103` instantiates this specialization for e4m3, with host entry `invokePerTokenQuantization` (`csrc/nv_internal/cpp/kernels/quantization.cu:242`). `invokePerTokenQuantization` has no callers in the repo, and the trtllm-gen MoE launcher accepts only MxFp8 and DeepSeekFp8 for fp8 (`csrc/trtllm_fused_moe_kernel_launcher.cu:1015`), so no shipped path reaches this cast today. The vendored tree itself is alive, though. The launcher includes its `quantization.h` (line 38), `csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/kernels/communicationKernels/moeAlltoAllKernels.cu` uses `quantization_utils.cuh`, and #5031 (open) fixes an NVFP4 accuracy regression inside this same vendored `quantization.cuh`. A change that wires the fp8 per-token path back, a new kernel added to this tree, or a downstream build that compiles `csrc/nv_internal` directly would inherit the double rounding.

### Measured impact

Hello，may i ask why nvfp4_compute_output_scale use twice rcp?
output_scale = global_scale/block_scale
this function first rcp.approx(global_scale) get 1/global_scale
then mul block_scale with 1/global_scale, get block_scale*(1/global_scale)
finally rcp(block_scale*(1/global_scale)), we get 1/(block_scale*(1/global_scale))
why not rcp(block_scale) and then mul global_scale?
What considerations are involved here? Thank you very much for taking the time to answer my question :)

```
@dsl_user_op
def nvfp4_compute_output_scale(
    fp8_val: Uint32, global_scale: Float32, *, loc=None, ip=None
) -> Float32:
    """Compute NVFP4 output_scale matching the CUDA kernel exactly.

    Converts E4M3 scale factor to float via hardware f16x2 path, then computes
    rcp(float_scale * rcp(global_scale)). Returns 0 when scale is zero.

    This matches quantization_utils.cuh:
        SFValue = static_cast<float>(tmp);
        outputScale = rcp_approx(SFValue * rcp_approx(SFScaleVal));
    """
    return Float32(
        llvm.inline_asm(
            T.f32(),
            [
                Uint32(fp8_val).ir_value(loc=loc, ip=ip),
                Float32(global_scale).ir_value(loc=loc, ip=ip),
            ],
            """
            {
                .reg .pred p_zero;
                .reg .b16 fp8_pair;
                .reg .b32 h2_32;
                .reg .b16 h_lo, h_hi;
                .reg .f32 scale_f32, rcp_gs, product, result;

                cvt.u16.u32 fp8_pair, $1;
                cvt.rn.f16x2.e4m3x2 h2_32, fp8_pair;
                mov.b32 {h_lo, h_hi}, h2_32;
                cvt.f32.f16 scale_f32, h_lo;

                rcp.approx.ftz.f32 rcp_gs, $2;
                mul.f32 product, scale_f32, rcp_gs;
                rcp.approx.ftz.f32 result, product;

                setp.eq.f32 p_zero, scale_f32, 0f00000000;
                selp.f32 $0, 0f00000000, result, p_zero;
            }
            """,
            "=f,r,f",
            has_side_effects=False,
            is_align_stack=False,
            asm_dialect=llvm.AsmDialect.AD_ATT,
        )
    )
```
